    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, the **Zurich Zebras** offense is anchored by **Yordan Alvarez** (76.88) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (74.81), both of whom offer the highest ceilings against right-handed starters. **Yordan Alvarez** remains an essential start, as his elite xwOBA of 0.450 against Bibee compensates for the park factor, while **Pete Crow-Armstrong** serves as our primary catalyst. With a 90% steal-rate expectation against Gausman, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** is a high-leverage play specifically because we are prioritizing SB velocity; his speed metrics combined with the leadoff spot make him our most valuable asset for category-chasing today. **Junior Caminero** (69.42) also draws a green light, benefiting from a soft matchup against Poulin (5.00 xERA) that provides a significant buffer against his slight park disadvantage.\n\nWhile **Christian Yelich** (58.43) finished just shy of the \"Strong\" threshold due to the unfavorable platoon disadvantage against P\u00e9rez, he remains our most interesting roster decision. He narrowly edged out our other bench depth options\u2014including **Gary Sanchez**, **Luke Raley**, and **Josh Smith**\u2014because his leadoff spot provides a volume floor that is difficult to ignore even in a platoon struggle. We are monitoring **Dillon Dingler** (54.64) closely; while his base projection is moderate, the combination of an aggressive base-stealing opportunity against Eisert and his placement in the #2 hole makes him a sneaky producer for Roto efficiency. Conversely, we must be wary of **Chase Meidroth** (45.87) facing the elite arm of Skubal; his score is suppressed by the high-skill opposition, making him the most likely candidate for a mid-game swap if his early at-bats prove unproductive. With **Trea Turner** and **Kyle Schwarber** off the slate, we are relying on the consistency of the outfield trio\u2014**Pete Crow-Armstrong**, **Lars Nootbaar**, and **Christian Yelich**\u2014to provide the necessary speed and power balance to keep us competitive in 5x5.",
